          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Shared UDS JSON listener preserves userId and admin authDetails/userId/clientId from Keycloak events. User and process/client activity can therefore be attributed within the identity service; this does not assert that every application-domain audit event has an actor.\n\nComponents: UDS Core 1.14 shared Keycloak realm and Horizon OIDC clients; no bundled application Keycloak\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-aaa-release] re-aaa-release\nSuccessful UDS publication artifact horizon-release-2026.45.4-uds.1 platform-contract.json selects identity.mode=uds, issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds, Core1.14.0; build-info.json enumerates12 deliveredimages and no bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-aaa-json-events] re-aaa-json-events\nListener serializes user/admin identity events to stdout with actor/source/time/detail, suitable for UDS Vector collection; does not itself pushSA/ISSO notification messages.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java\n\n[re-aaa-realm] re-aaa-realm\nRealm has failureFactor3, permanentLockout true, maxTemporaryLockouts0; password length15,upper/lower/digit,special2,history5 and expiry60days; jsonlog listener emits user/admin events. Browser has password and X509 alternative authentication; X509MFA defaultdisabled. Account-inactivity workflow optionaldisabled; no minimum-age/difference/blacklist policy selected. Recovery flow requires reset-password.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Shared UDS JSON listener preserves userId and admin authDetails/userId/clientId from Keycloak events. User and process/client activity can therefore be attributed within the identity service; this does not assert that every application-domain audit event has an actor.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204652",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204652",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000108",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204652",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000108",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204652r960912_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204652r960912",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000108-AAA-000290",
          "rule_title": "AAA Services must be configured to alert the SA and ISSO when any audit processing failure occurs.",
          "fix_text": "Configure AAA Services to alert the SA and ISSO when any audit processing failure occurs.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify AAA Services are configured to alert the SA and ISSO when any audit processing failure occurs.\n\nIf AAA Services are not configured to alert the SA and ISSO when any audit processing failure occurs, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "AAA_Services_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "It is critical for the appropriate personnel to be aware if a system is at risk of failing to process audit logs as required. Without this notification, the security personnel may be unaware of an impending failure of the audit capability and system operation may be adversely affected. \n\nAudit processing failures include software/hardware errors, failures in the audit capturing mechanisms, and audit storage capacity being reached or exceeded.\n\nThis requirement applies to each audit data storage repository (i.e., distinct information system component where audit records are stored), the centralized audit storage capacity of organizations (i.e., all audit data storage repositories combined), or both.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-95581",
            "V-80871"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000139"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2896",
          "uuid": "aade235c-4409-51cf-ba98-1d586740f69c",
          "stig_uuid": "48d52574-4cd5-5714-881e-63304d1f3ea9",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Monitoring and logging capability is partially implemented. UDS provides independent Vector/Loki collection and monitoring, but the reviewed default sharedIdP/alerting configuration does not establish audit-processing-failure delivery to designated SA/ISSO. Treat this as sharedplatform alert integration, not an API-specific notification implementation. Gallatin operates UDS monitoring and can enable shipped rules/receivers directly; DU managedclusters are a different authorization boundary. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the recommendation where existing capabilities need alerting configuration. Credit existing sources and monitoring; configure and verify the specific required alert coverage and recipient delivery. This does not establish that every required detector or alert rule is already present.\n\nComponents: UDS Core 1.14 shared Keycloak realm and Horizon OIDC clients; no bundled application Keycloak\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Identify the audit/log processing failures covered by this exact rule, including capture, hardware/software, collector/backend and storage failures as applicable. Configure the required alerts and named recipients using the existing central monitoring. Test coverage and delivery; verify real-time latency where the rule requires it. Retain event-source mappings, rules, routes and delivery-test evidence. Reuse shared monitoring rather than create a separate application alert engine.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-aaa-release] re-aaa-release\nSuccessful UDS publication artifact horizon-release-2026.45.4-uds.1 platform-contract.json selects identity.mode=uds, issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds, Core1.14.0; build-info.json enumerates12 deliveredimages and no bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-aaa-json-events] re-aaa-json-events\nListener serializes user/admin identity events to stdout with actor/source/time/detail, suitable for UDS Vector collection; does not itself pushSA/ISSO notification messages.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java\n\n[re-aaa-alerts] re-aaa-alerts\nOptional PrometheusRules alert on realm/user/admin modifications only if detailedObservability.alerts.enabled; rule availability is not configured delivery toSA/ISSO.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/keycloak-logging-alerting-rules.yaml\n\n[re-aaa-values] re-aaa-values\nCore uses identity-config0.32, X509/OTP enabled, username/password enabled; X509_MFA false, inactivity flag unset, detailed-observability alertingdisabled. Insecure admin password generation disabled. Truststore includes UDS bundle and serviceaccount CA separately from configimage truststore.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-alerts] mpn-identity-alerts\nSet detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=true to create alreadyshipped Keycloak realm/user/admin modificationPrometheusRules. Logging/dashboards alreadyenabled. This is GallatinCoreconfiguration, not DU featuredevelopment.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/how-to-guides/identity-and-authorization/configure-keycloak-notifications-and-alerts.mdx\n\n[mpn-identity-routes] mpn-identity-routes\nRoute via kube-prometheus-stack chart alertmanager.config.receivers/route, secretbacked notification credentials and uds-prometheus-config.additionalNetworkAllow matching endpoint. Gallatin must select actualSA/ISSO recipients; defaults cannot invent them.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/how-to-guides/monitoring-and-observability/route-alerts-to-notification-channels.mdx",
          "finding_details": "Monitoring and logging capability is partially implemented. UDS provides independent Vector/Loki collection and monitoring, but the reviewed default sharedIdP/alerting configuration does not establish audit-processing-failure delivery to designated SA/ISSO. Treat this as sharedplatform alert integration, not an API-specific notification implementation. Gallatin operates UDS monitoring and can enable shipped rules/receivers directly; DU managedclusters are a different authorization boundary. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the recommendation where existing capabilities need alerting configuration. Credit existing sources and monitoring; configure and verify the specific required alert coverage and recipient delivery. This does not establish that every required detector or alert rule is already present.\n\nNext action: Identify the audit/log processing failures covered by this exact rule, including capture, hardware/software, collector/backend and storage failures as applicable. Configure the required alerts and named recipients using the existing central monitoring. Test coverage and delivery; verify real-time latency where the rule requires it. Retain event-source mappings, rules, routes and delivery-test evidence. Reuse shared monitoring rather than create a separate application alert engine."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204655",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204655",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000116",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204655",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000116",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204655r960927_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204655r960927",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000116-AAA-000320",
          "rule_title": "AAA Services must be configured to use internal system clocks to generate time stamps for audit records.",
          "fix_text": "Configure AAA Services to use internal system clocks to generate time stamps for audit records.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify AAA Services are configured to use internal system clocks to generate time stamps for audit records.\n\nIf AAA Services are not configured to use internal system clocks to generate time stamps for audit records, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "AAA_Services_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Without an internal clock used as the reference for the time stored on each event to provide a trusted common reference for the time, forensic analysis would be impeded. Determining the correct time a particular event occurred on a system is critical when conducting forensic analysis and investigating system events. \n\nIf the internal clock is not used, the system may not be able to provide time stamps for log messages. Additionally, externally generated time stamps may not be accurate. Applications can use the capability of an operating system or purpose-built module for this purpose. Synchronizing the internal clock using NTP provides uniformity for all system clocks over a network. NTP provides an efficient and scalable method for network devices to synchronize to an accurate time source.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-95587",
            "V-80877"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000159"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2896",
          "uuid": "0e1b5e05-493b-5c8e-8fe4-7ac4e5041ed1",
          "stig_uuid": "48d52574-4cd5-5714-881e-63304d1f3ea9",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The selected provider emits timestamps from Keycloak event time on the container/node system clock; the listener formats that actual epoch value rather than using user-supplied timestamps. Host time synchronization is a distinct inherited obligation, not a reason to run NTP in each application container.\n\nComponents: UDS Core 1.14 shared Keycloak realm and Horizon OIDC clients; no bundled application Keycloak\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-aaa-release] re-aaa-release\nSuccessful UDS publication artifact horizon-release-2026.45.4-uds.1 platform-contract.json selects identity.mode=uds, issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds, Core1.14.0; build-info.json enumerates12 deliveredimages and no bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-aaa-json-events] re-aaa-json-events\nListener serializes user/admin identity events to stdout with actor/source/time/detail, suitable for UDS Vector collection; does not itself pushSA/ISSO notification messages.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java\n\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The selected provider emits timestamps from Keycloak event time on the container/node system clock; the listener formats that actual epoch value rather than using user-supplied timestamps. Host time synchronization is a distinct inherited obligation, not a reason to run NTP in each application container.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204656",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204656",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000141",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204656",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000141",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204656r960963_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204656r960963",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000141-AAA-000670",
          "rule_title": "AAA Services must be configured to disable non-essential modules.",
          "fix_text": "Configure AAA Services to disable non-essential modules.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Determine if AAA Services are configured to disable non-essential modules.\n\nIf AAA Services are not configured to disable non-essential modules, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "AAA_Services_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "It is detrimental for applications to provide, or install by default, functionality exceeding requirements or mission objectives. These unnecessary capabilities or services are often overlooked and therefore may remain unsecured. They increase the risk to the platform by providing additional attack vectors.\n\nApplications are capable of providing a wide variety of functions and services. Some of the functions and services, provided by default, may not be necessary to support essential organizational operations (e.g., key missions, functions). \n\nExamples of non-essential capabilities include, but are not limited to, advertising software or browser plug-ins not related to requirements or providing a wide array of functionality not required for every mission, but cannot be disabled.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-95655",
            "V-80945"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000381"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2896",
          "uuid": "d18e769c-7be6-5550-aa16-e6d71d88f564",
          "stig_uuid": "48d52574-4cd5-5714-881e-63304d1f3ea9",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual Core Keycloak exposes an explicit features.additional allow-list (empty by default) beyond required FIPS, configured identity/authentication flows and separated public/admin service interfaces. The shipped platform package supplies least-functionality controls. Keep the delivered feature inventory and any exceptions with the host/platform hardening record.\n\nComponents: UDS Core 1.14 shared Keycloak realm and Horizon OIDC clients; no bundled application Keycloak\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-aaa-release] re-aaa-release\nSuccessful UDS publication artifact horizon-release-2026.45.4-uds.1 platform-contract.json selects identity.mode=uds, issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds, Core1.14.0; build-info.json enumerates12 deliveredimages and no bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-aaa-values] re-aaa-values\nCore uses identity-config0.32, X509/OTP enabled, username/password enabled; X509_MFA false, inactivity flag unset, detailed-observability alertingdisabled. Insecure admin password generation disabled. Truststore includes UDS bundle and serviceaccount CA separately from configimage truststore.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[re-aaa-sso-boundary] re-aaa-sso-boundary\nPlatformKeycloak admin/public ingress is separate, sanitized clientcertificate header forwarded from gateway; certificate access available but optional at default browser boundary.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[re-aaa-security-isolation] re-aaa-security-isolation\nKeycloak runtime configured strictFIPS, dedicated serviceidentity and file/volume paths; sensitive data stored within protected service/persistent/externalDB boundary. OS/storage protection is inherited, not a requirement to add independent application encryption.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/statefulset.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Actual Core Keycloak exposes an explicit features.additional allow-list (empty by default) beyond required FIPS, configured identity/authentication flows and separated public/admin service interfaces. The shipped platform package supplies least-functionality controls. Keep the delivered feature inventory and any exceptions with the host/platform hardening record.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204657",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204657",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000142",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204657",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000142",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204657r1043177_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204657r1043177",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000142-AAA-000010",
          "rule_title": "AAA Services must be configured to use secure protocols when connecting to directory services.",
          "fix_text": "Configure AAA Services to use secure protocols when connecting to directory services. The use of LDAP over TLS (LDAPS) is the most common method to secure the directory services or user database traffic. However, proprietary or other protocols may be used in some configurations. Each protocol egressing the local enclave must be implemented in accordance with its PPSM CAL.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If AAA Services do not connect to a directory services or other identity provider, but instead perform user and device account management as part of their functionality, this is not applicable.\n\nReview the AAA Services configuration when connecting to directory services or another identity provider. Verify the connection is configured to use secure protocols for transport between AAA Services and the directory services using mutual authentication. The use of LDAP over TLS (LDAPS) is the most common method to secure the directory services or user database traffic. Each protocol egressing the local enclave must be implemented in accordance with its PPSM CAL.\n\nIf AAA Services do not use secure protocols when connecting to directory services, this is a finding. If the protocols are not implemented in accordance with the PPSM CAL, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "AAA_Services_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Authenticity protection provides protection against man-in-the-middle attacks/session hijacking and the insertion of false information into sessions.\n\nApplication communication sessions are protected utilizing transport encryption protocols, such as TLS. TLS provides a means to authenticate sessions and encrypt application traffic. Session authentication can be single (one-way) or mutual (two-way) in nature. Single authentication authenticates the server for the client, whereas mutual authentication provides a means for both the client and the server to authenticate each other. \n\nThis requirement addresses communications protection at the application session, versus the network packet, and establishes grounds for confidence at both ends of communications sessions in ongoing identities of other parties and in the validity of information transmitted.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-95525",
            "V-80815"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000382"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2896",
          "uuid": "d4a7b9a4-7405-5e55-86cb-18fd7615bfa4",
          "stig_uuid": "48d52574-4cd5-5714-881e-63304d1f3ea9",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs N/A justification\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: SharedUDS is the delivered IdP, not bundledHorizon. Default sharedUDS realm has no selected externalLDAP/directory federation; this check expressly exempts AAA services that manage accounts themselves. Horizon-to-UDS OIDC uses HTTPS and verified signedtokens, but is not evidence of an unseen IdP-to-LDAP connection. Record upstream directory/broker applicability separately and assess its secure mutuallyauthenticated transport if enabled.\n\nComponents: UDS Core 1.14 shared Keycloak realm and Horizon OIDC clients; no bundled application Keycloak\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Record absence/presence of upstream directory federation at sharedUDS; attach secure protocol/PPSM mapping for any actual federation.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-aaa-release] re-aaa-release\nSuccessful UDS publication artifact horizon-release-2026.45.4-uds.1 platform-contract.json selects identity.mode=uds, issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds, Core1.14.0; build-info.json enumerates12 deliveredimages and no bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-aaa-realm] re-aaa-realm\nRealm has failureFactor3, permanentLockout true, maxTemporaryLockouts0; password length15,upper/lower/digit,special2,history5 and expiry60days; jsonlog listener emits user/admin events. Browser has password and X509 alternative authentication; X509MFA defaultdisabled. Account-inactivity workflow optionaldisabled; no minimum-age/difference/blacklist policy selected. Recovery flow requires reset-password.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-aaa-values] re-aaa-values\nCore uses identity-config0.32, X509/OTP enabled, username/password enabled; X509_MFA false, inactivity flag unset, detailed-observability alertingdisabled. Insecure admin password generation disabled. Truststore includes UDS bundle and serviceaccount CA separately from configimage truststore.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Needs N/A justification. SharedUDS is the delivered IdP, not bundledHorizon. Default sharedUDS realm has no selected externalLDAP/directory federation; this check expressly exempts AAA services that manage accounts themselves. Horizon-to-UDS OIDC uses HTTPS and verified signedtokens, but is not evidence of an unseen IdP-to-LDAP connection. Record upstream directory/broker applicability separately and assess its secure mutuallyauthenticated transport if enabled.\n\nNext action: Record absence/presence of upstream directory federation at sharedUDS; attach secure protocol/PPSM mapping for any actual federation."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204658",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204658",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000142",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204658",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000142",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204658r1043177_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204658r1043177",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000142-AAA-000020",
          "rule_title": "AAA Services must be configured to use protocols that encrypt credentials when authenticating clients, as defined in the PPSM CAL and vulnerability assessments.",
          "fix_text": "Configure AAA Services to use protocols that encrypt credentials when authenticating clients. Both the RADIUS and TACACS+ protocols are acceptable when configured to perform encryption. For any protocol implemented, the PPSM CAL and vulnerability assessments must be reviewed to ensure the protocols are properly configured.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify AAA Services are configured to use protocols that encrypt credentials when authenticating clients. Both the RADIUS and TACACS+ protocols are acceptable when configured to perform encryption. For any protocol implemented, the PPSM CAL and vulnerability assessments must be reviewed to ensure the protocols are properly configured.\n\nIf AAA Services are not configured to use protocols that encrypt credentials when authenticating clients, as defined in the PPSM CAL and vulnerability assessments, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "AAA_Services_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Authentication protection of the client credentials (specifically the password or shared secret) prevents unauthorized access to resources. The RADIUS protocol encrypts the password field in the access-request packet, from the client to the AAA server. The remainder of the packet is unencrypted. Other information, such as username, authorized services, and accounting, can be captured by a third-party. TACACS+ encrypts the entire body of the packet but leaves a standard TACACS+ header. Within the header is a field that indicates whether the body is encrypted or not. Other protocols have similar protections. When unencrypted credentials are passed, adversaries can gain access to resources.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-95527",
            "V-80817"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000382"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2896",
          "uuid": "5a508f75-e2db-5e48-8e75-3b468874da79",
          "stig_uuid": "48d52574-4cd5-5714-881e-63304d1f3ea9",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual Horizon shared-identity issuer is HTTPS at sso.uds.dev. Core gateway enforces protected HTTPS ingress and STRICT mesh mTLS protects enrolled service hops, so credentials are protected by the transport layer even though Keycloak listens HTTP behind the gateway. Formal PPSM approval is retained separately; application-local TLS is not required on top of the protected mesh.\n\nComponents: UDS Core 1.14 shared Keycloak realm and Horizon OIDC clients; no bundled application Keycloak\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-aaa-release] re-aaa-release\nSuccessful UDS publication artifact horizon-release-2026.45.4-uds.1 platform-contract.json selects identity.mode=uds, issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds, Core1.14.0; build-info.json enumerates12 deliveredimages and no bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[core-gateway] core-gateway\nGateway redirects HTTP to HTTPS and requires TLS 1.3 by default, optionally TLS 1.2; credentialName references Kubernetes TLS secrets.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/charts/uds-istio-config/templates/gateway.yaml\n\n[core-mtls] core-mtls\nRoot-namespace PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS. Horizon opts into ambient mesh; in-mesh plaintext-looking URLs are protected by mesh rather than application TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml\n\n[re-aaa-sso-boundary] re-aaa-sso-boundary\nPlatformKeycloak admin/public ingress is separate, sanitized clientcertificate header forwarded from gateway; certificate access available but optional at default browser boundary.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Actual Horizon shared-identity issuer is HTTPS at sso.uds.dev. Core gateway enforces protected HTTPS ingress and STRICT mesh mTLS protects enrolled service hops, so credentials are protected by the transport layer even though Keycloak listens HTTP behind the gateway. Formal PPSM approval is retained separately; application-local TLS is not required on top of the protected mesh.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204659",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204659",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000142",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204659",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000142",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204659r1043177_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204659r1043177",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000142-AAA-000680",
          "rule_title": "AAA Services must be configured to prohibit or restrict the use of organization-defined functions, ports, protocols, and/or services, as defined in the PPSM CAL and vulnerability assessments.",
          "fix_text": "Configure AAA Services to prohibit or restrict the use of organization-defined functions, ports, protocols, and/or services, as defined in the PPSM CAL and vulnerability assessments.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the AAA Services configuration to ascertain if it prohibits or restricts the use of organization-defined functions, ports, protocols, and/or services. Further determine if the use is as defined in the PPSM CAL and vulnerability assessments.\n\nIf AAA Services are not configured in accordance with the PPSM CAL and vulnerability assessments, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "AAA_Services_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "In order to prevent unauthorized connection of devices, unauthorized transfer of information, or unauthorized tunneling (i.e., embedding of data types within data types), organizations must disable or restrict unused or unnecessary physical and logical ports/protocols on information systems.\n\nApplications are capable of providing a wide variety of functions and services. Some of the functions and services provided by default may not be necessary to support essential organizational operations. Additionally, it is sometimes convenient to provide multiple services from a single component (e.g., email and web services; however, doing so increases risk over limiting the services provided by any one component. \n\nTo support the requirements and principles of least functionality, the application must support the organizational requirements providing only essential capabilities and limiting the use of ports, protocols, and/or services to only those required, authorized, and approved to conduct official business or to address authorized quality of life issues.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-95657",
            "V-80947"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000382"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2896",
          "uuid": "ea8fab32-c2e7-5f6f-a7bc-4433f98568db",
          "stig_uuid": "48d52574-4cd5-5714-881e-63304d1f3ea9",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The actual UDS Keycloak Package declares public/admin endpoints and explicit allowed service flows under generated default-deny policy. Horizon release pins the shared-identity host/443 boundary; restrictions are supplied by UDS operator/network policy rather than duplicated in Keycloak application code. Final PPSM approval must identify these actual flows.\n\nComponents: UDS Core 1.14 shared Keycloak realm and Horizon OIDC clients; no bundled application Keycloak\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Attach the final approved identity-service PPSM/flow mapping; keep shared UDS network policy inheritance explicit.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-aaa-release] re-aaa-release\nSuccessful UDS publication artifact horizon-release-2026.45.4-uds.1 platform-contract.json selects identity.mode=uds, issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds, Core1.14.0; build-info.json enumerates12 deliveredimages and no bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts\n\n[re-aaa-sso-boundary] re-aaa-sso-boundary\nPlatformKeycloak admin/public ingress is separate, sanitized clientcertificate header forwarded from gateway; certificate access available but optional at default browser boundary.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The actual UDS Keycloak Package declares public/admin endpoints and explicit allowed service flows under generated default-deny policy. Horizon release pins the shared-identity host/443 boundary; restrictions are supplied by UDS operator/network policy rather than duplicated in Keycloak application code. Final PPSM approval must identify these actual flows.\n\nNext action: Attach the final approved identity-service PPSM/flow mapping; keep shared UDS network policy inheritance explicit."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204660",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204660",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000148",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204660",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000148",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204660r1051115_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204660r1051115",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000148-AAA-000390",
          "rule_title": "AAA Services must be configured to uniquely identify and authenticate organizational users.",
          "fix_text": "Configure AAA Services to uniquely identify and authenticate organizational users.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify AAA Services are configured to uniquely identify and authenticate organizational users. For STIGs produced from this requirement, when AAA Services are used to authenticate processes acting on behalf of organizational users, they also must be uniquely identified and authenticated.\n\nIf AAA Services are not configured to uniquely identify and authenticate organizational users, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "AAA_Services_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "To assure accountability and prevent unauthenticated access, organizational users must be identified and authenticated to prevent potential misuse and compromise of the system. \n\nOrganizational users include organizational employees or individuals the organization deems to have equivalent status of employees (e.g., contractors). Organizational users (and any processes acting on behalf of users) must be uniquely identified and authenticated for all accesses, except the following.\n\n(i) Accesses explicitly identified and documented by the organization. Organizations document specific user actions that can be performed on the information system without identification or authentication; and \n(ii) Accesses that occur through authorized use of group authenticators without individual authentication. Organizations may require unique identification of individuals in group accounts (e.g., shared privilege accounts) or for detailed accountability of individual activity.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-95601",
            "V-80891"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000764"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2896",
          "uuid": "ddf450a0-65f6-5d9b-a828-9f82aede947c",
          "stig_uuid": "48d52574-4cd5-5714-881e-63304d1f3ea9",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual released Horizon uses the shared UDS realm and signed OIDC subject identities; identity userIds are individual and tenant groups grant authorization rather than act as shared logins. Provider authentication precedes access to shared application resources. No bundled bootstrap administrator is delivered.\n\nComponents: UDS Core 1.14 shared Keycloak realm and Horizon OIDC clients; no bundled application Keycloak\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-aaa-release] re-aaa-release\nSuccessful UDS publication artifact horizon-release-2026.45.4-uds.1 platform-contract.json selects identity.mode=uds, issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds, Core1.14.0; build-info.json enumerates12 deliveredimages and no bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-aaa-realm] re-aaa-realm\nRealm has failureFactor3, permanentLockout true, maxTemporaryLockouts0; password length15,upper/lower/digit,special2,history5 and expiry60days; jsonlog listener emits user/admin events. Browser has password and X509 alternative authentication; X509MFA defaultdisabled. Account-inactivity workflow optionaldisabled; no minimum-age/difference/blacklist policy selected. Recovery flow requires reset-password.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-aaa-values] re-aaa-values\nCore uses identity-config0.32, X509/OTP enabled, username/password enabled; X509_MFA false, inactivity flag unset, detailed-observability alertingdisabled. Insecure admin password generation disabled. Truststore includes UDS bundle and serviceaccount CA separately from configimage truststore.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[authz] Application access control\nAPI guard validates identity and sets tenant/user context; permission guard invokes a permission enforcer when route metadata is present. This is source support, not complete route/tenant coverage.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Actual released Horizon uses the shared UDS realm and signed OIDC subject identities; identity userIds are individual and tenant groups grant authorization rather than act as shared logins. Provider authentication precedes access to shared application resources. No bundled bootstrap administrator is delivered.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204661",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204661",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000149",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204661",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000149",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204661r960972_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204661r960972",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000149-AAA-000400",
          "rule_title": "AAA Services must be configured to require multifactor authentication using Personal Identity Verification (PIV) credentials for authenticating privileged user accounts.",
          "fix_text": "Configure AAA Services to require multifactor authentication using PIV credentials for authenticating privileged user accounts. Although the CAC is a PIV credential, it should not be used for privileged accounts, but rather only for non-privileged accounts.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify AAA Services are configured to require multifactor authentication using PIV credentials for authenticating privileged user accounts. Although the Common Access Card (CAC) is a PIV credential, it should not be used for privileged accounts, but rather only for non-privileged accounts. Administrative smart cards and tokens, separate from the CAC, are the preferred solution for privileged accounts.\n\nIf AAA Services are not configured to require multifactor authentication using PIV credentials for authenticating privileged user accounts, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "AAA_Services_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Without the use of multifactor authentication, the ease of access to privileged functions is greatly increased. \n\nMultifactor authentication requires using two or more factors to achieve authentication. \n\nFactors include: \n(i) something a user knows (e.g., password/PIN); \n(ii) something a user has (e.g., cryptographic identification device, token); or \n(iii) something a user is (e.g., biometric). \n\nA privileged account is defined as an information system account with authorizations of a privileged user. \n\nNetwork access is defined as access to an information system by a user (or a process acting on behalf of a user) communicating through a network (e.g., local area network, wide area network, or the Internet).",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-95603",
            "V-80893"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000765"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2896",
          "uuid": "8dbce3ba-30ce-5ccc-9ce5-8251a5474e36",
          "stig_uuid": "48d52574-4cd5-5714-881e-63304d1f3ea9",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Shared UDS supplies CAC/PIV/X509 mechanisms. However default browser flow also allows username/password, the password branch requires OTP via chart-generated MFA_FLOW_ENABLED/OTP_FLOW_ENABLED=REQUIRED, while X509_MFA_ENABLED=false; no deliveredprofile requires smartcard authentication for the applicable privileged/nonprivileged paths. Required PIV/CAC enforcement remains a provider-policy choice, not missing application code. Core implements CAC/PIV/X509 and required OTP on password paths, but defaults still permit password alternatives. Gallatin owns the required-card flow policy.\n\nComponents: UDS Core 1.14 shared Keycloak realm and Horizon OIDC clients; no bundled application Keycloak\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Gallatin configures the approved required-card authentication path in Core: X509_AUTH_ENABLED=true and, for a certificate-only policy, USERNAME_PASSWORD_AUTH_ENABLED=false and SOCIAL_AUTH_ENABLED=false; apply equivalent existing-realm flow changes through controlled AdminConsole/API. Preserve approved emergency access and distinguish privileged PIV-card eligibility from personal CAC. These are existing provider flows, not new Horizon authentication features.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-aaa-release] re-aaa-release\nSuccessful UDS publication artifact horizon-release-2026.45.4-uds.1 platform-contract.json selects identity.mode=uds, issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds, Core1.14.0; build-info.json enumerates12 deliveredimages and no bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-aaa-realm] re-aaa-realm\nRealm has failureFactor3, permanentLockout true, maxTemporaryLockouts0; password length15,upper/lower/digit,special2,history5 and expiry60days; jsonlog listener emits user/admin events. Browser has password and X509 alternative authentication; X509MFA defaultdisabled. Account-inactivity workflow optionaldisabled; no minimum-age/difference/blacklist policy selected. Recovery flow requires reset-password.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-aaa-values] re-aaa-values\nCore uses identity-config0.32, X509/OTP enabled, username/password enabled; X509_MFA false, inactivity flag unset, detailed-observability alertingdisabled. Insecure admin password generation disabled. Truststore includes UDS bundle and serviceaccount CA separately from configimage truststore.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[re-aaa-sso-boundary] re-aaa-sso-boundary\nPlatformKeycloak admin/public ingress is separate, sanitized clientcertificate header forwarded from gateway; certificate access available but optional at default browser boundary.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[re-aaa-flow-env] re-aaa-flow-env\nDefault OTP_ENABLED=true generates OTP_FLOW_ENABLED and MFA_FLOW_ENABLED REQUIRED; X509 remains ALTERNATIVE and X509_MFA disabled. Do not infer OTPbypass solely from conditional-flow name or CONFIGURE_TOTP defaultActionfalse.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/secret-kc-realm.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-mapping] mpn-identity-mapping\nrealmInitEnv keys are prefixed REALM_ into realm import environment; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS>0 sets enabled true. realmAuthFlows controls required/disabled MFA/password/X509 execution variables. No further vendor service is needed to set these controls.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/secret-kc-realm.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-init-only] mpn-identity-init-only\nrealmInitEnv and realmAuthFlows are initial realm import inputs. Existing realm is persisted in DB; redeploying Secret does not change it. Core documents equivalent Keycloak AdminConsole settings. Use controlled non-destructive AdminAPI/console update and persist the desired bundle override; do not advise tearing down production identity.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/how-to-guides/identity-and-authorization/configure-keycloak-login-policies.mdx",
          "finding_details": "Shared UDS supplies CAC/PIV/X509 mechanisms. However default browser flow also allows username/password, the password branch requires OTP via chart-generated MFA_FLOW_ENABLED/OTP_FLOW_ENABLED=REQUIRED, while X509_MFA_ENABLED=false; no deliveredprofile requires smartcard authentication for the applicable privileged/nonprivileged paths. Required PIV/CAC enforcement remains a provider-policy choice, not missing application code. Core implements CAC/PIV/X509 and required OTP on password paths, but defaults still permit password alternatives. Gallatin owns the required-card flow policy.\n\nNext action: Gallatin configures the approved required-card authentication path in Core: X509_AUTH_ENABLED=true and, for a certificate-only policy, USERNAME_PASSWORD_AUTH_ENABLED=false and SOCIAL_AUTH_ENABLED=false; apply equivalent existing-realm flow changes through controlled AdminConsole/API. Preserve approved emergency access and distinguish privileged PIV-card eligibility from personal CAC. These are existing provider flows, not new Horizon authentication features."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204662",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204662",
