          ],
          "group_id": "V-204671",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000171",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204671r981567_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204671r981567",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000171-AAA-000510",
          "rule_title": "For password-based authentication, AAA Services must be configured to store passwords using an approved salted key derivation function, preferably using a keyed hash.",
          "fix_text": "Configure AAA Services to encrypt locally stored credentials using a FIPS-validated cryptographic module.\n\nConfigure all associated databases, configuration files, and audit files to use only encrypted representations for all passwords so that no password strings are readable/discernable.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Where passwords are used, verify AAA Services are configured to encrypt locally stored credentials using a FIPS-validated cryptographic module. AAA Services may leverage the capability of an operating system or purpose-built module for this purpose. \n\nConfirm databases, configuration files, and log files have encrypted representations for all passwords, and that no password strings are readable/discernable. Potential locations include the local file system where configurations and events are stored, or in a related database table.\n\nReview AAA Services configuration for use of the MD5 algorithm to create password hashes.\n\nIf AAA Services are not configured to encrypt locally stored credentials using a FIPS-validated cryptographic module, this is a finding.\n\nIf AAA Services are configured to use MD5 to create password hashes, this is a finding.\n\nNote: FIPS-validated cryptographic modules are listed on the NIST Cryptographic Module Validation Program's (CMVP) validation list.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "AAA_Services_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Passwords must be protected at all times, and encryption is the standard method for protecting passwords. If passwords are not encrypted, they can be plainly read (i.e., clear text) and easily compromised.\n\nAAA Services must enforce cryptographic representations of passwords when storing passwords in databases, configuration files, and log files. Passwords must be protected at all times. Using a strong, one-way hashing encryption algorithm with a salt is the standard method for providing a means to validate a password without having to store the actual password.\n\nPerformance and time required to access are factors that must be considered, and the one-way hash is the most feasible means of securing the password and providing an acceptable measure of password security. If passwords are stored in clear text, they can be plainly read and easily compromised.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-95663",
            "V-80953"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004062"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2896",
          "uuid": "955a58b8-2e68-5d1d-b05c-21b1220a267f",
          "stig_uuid": "48d52574-4cd5-5714-881e-63304d1f3ea9",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Delivered release uses sharedUDS Keycloak, whose passwordPolicy selects PBKDF2-SHA256 rather than unused bundledPBKDF2-SHA512. Corestatefulset enables FIPS strict mode and identityconfig supplies FIPS providers. This supplies saltedKDF/approvedmode mechanism; modulecertificate/version mapping is handled by release crypto evidence, not inferred from an unrelated image name.\n\nComponents: UDS Core 1.14 shared Keycloak realm and Horizon OIDC clients; no bundled application Keycloak\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain delivered identity profile and provider boundary in final component checklist. Gallatin operates the selected Core identity service on MPN; the referenced Core source supplies known defaults, not a dependency on DU managedclusters.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-aaa-release] re-aaa-release\nSuccessful UDS publication artifact horizon-release-2026.45.4-uds.1 platform-contract.json selects identity.mode=uds, issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds, Core1.14.0; build-info.json enumerates12 deliveredimages and no bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-aaa-realm] re-aaa-realm\nRealm has failureFactor3, permanentLockout true, maxTemporaryLockouts0; password length15,upper/lower/digit,special2,history5 and expiry60days; jsonlog listener emits user/admin events. Browser has password and X509 alternative authentication; X509MFA defaultdisabled. Account-inactivity workflow optionaldisabled; no minimum-age/difference/blacklist policy selected. Recovery flow requires reset-password.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-aaa-security-isolation] re-aaa-security-isolation\nKeycloak runtime configured strictFIPS, dedicated serviceidentity and file/volume paths; sensitive data stored within protected service/persistent/externalDB boundary. OS/storage protection is inherited, not a requirement to add independent application encryption.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/statefulset.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Delivered release uses sharedUDS Keycloak, whose passwordPolicy selects PBKDF2-SHA256 rather than unused bundledPBKDF2-SHA512. Corestatefulset enables FIPS strict mode and identityconfig supplies FIPS providers. This supplies saltedKDF/approvedmode mechanism; modulecertificate/version mapping is handled by release crypto evidence, not inferred from an unrelated image name.\n\nNext action: Retain delivered identity profile and provider boundary in final component checklist. Gallatin operates the selected Core identity service on MPN; the referenced Core source supplies known defaults, not a dependency on DU managedclusters."
        },
