          "reference_identifier": "2896",
          "uuid": "1931b965-6e0a-5831-b869-08b57aae1b99",
          "stig_uuid": "48d52574-4cd5-5714-881e-63304d1f3ea9",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual published platform contract selects UDS shared identity, not bundled Keycloak. Its Core1.14 chart unconditionally enables FIPS strict mode, and identity-config supplies required BC-FIPS provider libraries; upstream confirms strict mode uses approved-mode crypto. Auth/session creation belongs to that provider; API token verification also runs in the actual CMVP5132 node-fips runtime. No duplicate API/nginx session generator or authentication crypto implementation is needed. The pure-JavaScript NATS signature exception is recorded separately under V204758 rather than incorrectly failing delegated UDS login/session generation.\n\nComponents: UDS Core 1.14 shared Keycloak realm and Horizon OIDC clients; no bundled application Keycloak\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the exact delivered-release mapping and cited evidence with the checklist; no additional application change recommended.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-images-release] re-images-release\nSuccessful Nov5 trusted release2026.45.4-uds.1 binds Horizonb84b9af/build19710/run42109769948, package d528b7f, Core1.14.0, actual identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. Twelve delivered image platform digests are explicit; no bundled Keycloak is shipped. Published package digest sha256:486f55939f94ab081d2a12ecade5439512f8f7c4b3119c40b027c711d386adc9. Read build-info/platform-contract/publication metadata artifact13464284834, not just example chart defaults.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-images-core-identity] re-images-core-identity\nCore1.14 always starts Keycloak --features=fips --fips-mode=strict. identity-config0.32 stages bc-fips/bctls/bcutil jars via keycloak-crypto-fips1402 and syncs these providers; supported hardened flavors exist. Actual contract selects this shared identity, so web/API session and authentication crypto belongs here, not missing bundled Keycloak settings. Actual Horizon contract does not select the Core distribution flavor.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/statefulset.yaml\n\n[re-images-identity-libs] re-images-identity-libs\nIdentity-config stages required BouncyCastle FIPS provider jars from its version-matched Keycloak FIPS dependency; sync.sh copies these into Keycloak provider directory when FIPS_ENABLED.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/Dockerfile\n\n[re-images-keycloak-fips] re-images-keycloak-fips\nUpstream documents that --features=fips --fips-mode=strict uses BouncyCastle FIPS approved mode; session generation/authentication crypto can be delegated to the actual UDS identity service.\nhttps://www.keycloak.org/server/fips\n\n[re-images-api-sbom] re-images-api-sbom\nActual built API SBOM contains nodejs24.22.1-r2, NIST-CMVP-5132, openssl-provider-fips-3.4.0 version3.4.0-r5, openssl-config-fipshardened4.1.0-r0. NIST-CMVP-5523-optin is also present but is not the default provider.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/actions/runs/42109769948/artifacts/13453332302\n\n[re-images-node-mode] re-images-node-mode\nVendor explicitly says node-fips Crypto APIs are configured into approved mode at image build time through openssl-config-fipshardened, without application code changes.\nhttps://images.chainguard.dev/directory/image/node-fips/overview\n\n[re-images-nist5132] re-images-nist5132\nNIST lists Chainguard FIPS Provider for OpenSSL, FIPS140-3 certificate5132, Active, sunset2031-01-13, approved-mode caveat. Exact API/web/Postgres SBOM markers map to this certificate; no certificate number guessed from generic FIPS naming.\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/projects/cryptographic-module-validation-program/certificate/5132\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Actual published platform contract selects UDS shared identity, not bundled Keycloak. Its Core1.14 chart unconditionally enables FIPS strict mode, and identity-config supplies required BC-FIPS provider libraries; upstream confirms strict mode uses approved-mode crypto. Auth/session creation belongs to that provider; API token verification also runs in the actual CMVP5132 node-fips runtime. No duplicate API/nginx session generator or authentication crypto implementation is needed. The pure-JavaScript NATS signature exception is recorded separately under V204758 rather than incorrectly failing delegated UDS login/session generation.\n\nNext action: Retain the exact delivered-release mapping and cited evidence with the checklist; no additional application change recommended."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204673",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204673",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000173",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204673",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000173",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204673r981570_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204673r981570",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000173-AAA-000530",
          "rule_title": "AAA Services must be configured to enforce 24 hours as the minimum password lifetime.",
          "fix_text": "Configure AAA Services to enforce 24 hours as the minimum password lifetime. When the AAA Services configuration setting is for \"1 day\", it is required that the length be 24 hours. This requirement is not applicable to service account passwords (e.g., shared secrets, pre-shared keys) or the account of last resort.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If AAA Services rely on directory services for user account management, this is not applicable and the connected directory services must perform this function. This requirement is not applicable to service account passwords (e.g., shared secrets, pre-shared keys) or the account of last resort.\n\nWhere passwords are used, such as temporary or emergency accounts, verify AAA Services are configured to enforce 24 hours as the minimum password lifetime. When the AAA Services configuration setting is for \"1 day\", it is required that the length be 24 hours.\n\nIf AAA Services are not configured to enforce 24 hours as the minimum password lifetime, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "AAA_Services_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Enforcing a minimum password lifetime helps prevent repeated password changes to defeat the password reuse or history enforcement requirement.\n\nRestricting this setting limits the user's ability to change their password. Passwords need to be changed at specific policy based intervals; however, if the application allows the user to immediately and continually change their password, then the password could be repeatedly changed in a short period of time to defeat the organization's policy regarding password reuse.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-95627",
            "V-80917"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004066"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2896",
          "uuid": "ff64e8f0-547f-530b-ae0a-740df77c9017",
          "stig_uuid": "48d52574-4cd5-5714-881e-63304d1f3ea9",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The actual UDS policy provides length/classes/history/60-day expiry, but neither eight-character password change-distance nor24-hour minimum-age enforcement. Passwordhistory does not enforce these distinct controls. Resolve policy/tailoring centrally for applicable human passwords; service/lastresort exemptions remain as the official check permits. This is a known provider policy gap/tailoring decision, not missing proof of an existing Core flag or a reason to duplicate identity in Horizon. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the same decision to similar password-change requirements. Share V-222544 password-scope verification and central policy enforcement; existing complexity/history controls do not establish this exact requirement. Preserve component-specific exceptions and do not infer N/A merely because the application delegates passwords to UDS.\n\nComponents: UDS Core 1.14 shared Keycloak realm and Horizon OIDC clients; no bundled application Keycloak\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Confirm password-authentication scope, current effective policy and the responsible identity/directory provider for this assessed component. Share V-222544 verification and enforce a 24-hour minimum password lifetime centrally where passwords remain in use and the control is not already supplied. Verify the exact rejection behavior and retain evidence. Evaluate no-password or delegated-directory applicability only under the exact rule and actual configuration. Do not invent an unsupported Core flag or duplicate identity management in Horizon. Historical-password reuse age is not the minimum interval before a new password change.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-aaa-release] re-aaa-release\nSuccessful UDS publication artifact horizon-release-2026.45.4-uds.1 platform-contract.json selects identity.mode=uds, issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds, Core1.14.0; build-info.json enumerates12 deliveredimages and no bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-aaa-realm] re-aaa-realm\nRealm has failureFactor3, permanentLockout true, maxTemporaryLockouts0; password length15,upper/lower/digit,special2,history5 and expiry60days; jsonlog listener emits user/admin events. Browser has password and X509 alternative authentication; X509MFA defaultdisabled. Account-inactivity workflow optionaldisabled; no minimum-age/difference/blacklist policy selected. Recovery flow requires reset-password.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-aaa-values] re-aaa-values\nCore uses identity-config0.32, X509/OTP enabled, username/password enabled; X509_MFA false, inactivity flag unset, detailed-observability alertingdisabled. Insecure admin password generation disabled. Truststore includes UDS bundle and serviceaccount CA separately from configimage truststore.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-realm] mpn-identity-realm\nKnowndefaults: idle600sec,maxlifespan36000sec; sessionlimit0; permanentlockout threshold3; default passwords length15/classes/history5/expiry60. CRL cachechecking disabled, OCSP checking enabled/failclosed; no eightcharacter-distance/24h minchange interval/selecteddenylist. Explicit source gaps are config/tailoring work, not Unknown.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[mpn-identity-age] mpn-identity-age\nNative passwordAge policy is historical-password reuse-age validation, not24-hour minimum time before changing to a differentnewpassword. Do not falsely propose passwordAge(1) for minchangeinterval; no Coredefault/customvalidator implementing8characterdistance/24hminchange supplied.\nhttps://github.com/keycloak/keycloak/blob/26.7.4/server-spi-private/src/main/java/org/keycloak/policy/AgePasswordPolicyProvider.java",
          "finding_details": "The actual UDS policy provides length/classes/history/60-day expiry, but neither eight-character password change-distance nor24-hour minimum-age enforcement. Passwordhistory does not enforce these distinct controls. Resolve policy/tailoring centrally for applicable human passwords; service/lastresort exemptions remain as the official check permits. This is a known provider policy gap/tailoring decision, not missing proof of an existing Core flag or a reason to duplicate identity in Horizon. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the same decision to similar password-change requirements. Share V-222544 password-scope verification and central policy enforcement; existing complexity/history controls do not establish this exact requirement. Preserve component-specific exceptions and do not infer N/A merely because the application delegates passwords to UDS.\n\nNext action: Confirm password-authentication scope, current effective policy and the responsible identity/directory provider for this assessed component. Share V-222544 verification and enforce a 24-hour minimum password lifetime centrally where passwords remain in use and the control is not already supplied. Verify the exact rejection behavior and retain evidence. Evaluate no-password or delegated-directory applicability only under the exact rule and actual configuration. Do not invent an unsupported Core flag or duplicate identity management in Horizon. Historical-password reuse age is not the minimum interval before a new password change."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204674",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204674",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000174",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204674",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000174",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204674r1267973_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204674r1267973",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000174-AAA-000540",
          "rule_title": "AAA Services must be configured to enforce a 180-day maximum password lifetime restriction.",
          "fix_text": "Configure AAA Services to enforce a 180-day maximum password lifetime restriction. Additionally, configure AAA Services to force password change upon the first logon after the expiration of the 180 days. This requirement is not applicable to service account passwords (e.g. shared secrets, pre-shared keys) or the account of last resort.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If AAA Services rely on directory services for user account management, this is not applicable and the connected directory services must perform this function. This requirement is not applicable to service account passwords (e.g. shared secrets, pre-shared keys) or the account of last resort.\n\nWhere passwords are used, such as temporary or emergency accounts, verify AAA Services are configured to enforce a 180-day maximum password lifetime restriction. Additionally, AAA Services must force password change upon the first logon after the expiration of the 180 days.\n\nIf AAA Services are not configured to enforce a 180-day maximum password lifetime restriction, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "AAA_Services_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Any password, no matter how complex, can eventually be cracked; therefore, passwords must be changed at specific intervals. \n\nOne method of minimizing this risk is to use complex passwords and periodically change them. If the application does not limit the lifetime of passwords and force users to change their passwords, there is the risk that the system and/or application passwords could be compromised. \n\nThis requirement does not include emergency administration accounts that are meant for access to the application in case of failure. These accounts are not required to have maximum password lifetime restrictions.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-95629",
            "V-80919"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004066"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2896",
          "uuid": "d3e0df78-1247-5e4e-b62f-be448b06c9bd",
          "stig_uuid": "48d52574-4cd5-5714-881e-63304d1f3ea9",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual sharedUDS passwordPolicy forceExpiredPasswordChange60 is stricter than the180-day maximum. Provider reset/update flow changes expired credentials before normal access. No API password-expiry implementation is needed.\n\nComponents: UDS Core 1.14 shared Keycloak realm and Horizon OIDC clients; no bundled application Keycloak\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-aaa-release] re-aaa-release\nSuccessful UDS publication artifact horizon-release-2026.45.4-uds.1 platform-contract.json selects identity.mode=uds, issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds, Core1.14.0; build-info.json enumerates12 deliveredimages and no bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-aaa-realm] re-aaa-realm\nRealm has failureFactor3, permanentLockout true, maxTemporaryLockouts0; password length15,upper/lower/digit,special2,history5 and expiry60days; jsonlog listener emits user/admin events. Browser has password and X509 alternative authentication; X509MFA defaultdisabled. Account-inactivity workflow optionaldisabled; no minimum-age/difference/blacklist policy selected. Recovery flow requires reset-password.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-aaa-recovery-ui] re-aaa-recovery-ui\nRecovery/update-password UI requires new password and confirmation; renders provider policy errors and defaults logout-other-sessions checked.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/theme/login/login-update-password.ftl\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Actual sharedUDS passwordPolicy forceExpiredPasswordChange60 is stricter than the180-day maximum. Provider reset/update flow changes expired credentials before normal access. No API password-expiry implementation is needed.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204675",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204675",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000175",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204675",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000175",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204675r1267976_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204675r1267976",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000175-AAA-000570",
          "rule_title": "AAA Services must be configured to only accept certificates issued by a DoW-approved certificate authority (CA) for Public Key Infrastructure (PKI)-based authentication.",
          "fix_text": "Configure AAA Services to only accept certificates issued by a DoW-approved CA for PKI-based authentication.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify AAA Services are configured to only accept certificates issued by a DoW-approved CA for PKI-based authentication.\n\nIf AAA Services are not configured to only accept certificates issued by a DoW-approved CA, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "AAA_Services_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Without path validation, an informed trust decision by the relying party cannot be made when presented with any certificate not already explicitly trusted.\n\nA trust anchor is an authoritative entity represented via a public key and associated data. It is used in the context of public key infrastructures, X.509 digital certificates, and DNSSEC. \n\nWhen there is a chain of trust, usually the top entity to be trusted becomes the trust anchor; it can be, for example, a Certification Authority (CA). A certification path starts with the subject certificate and proceeds through a number of intermediate certificates up to a trusted root certificate, typically issued by a trusted CA. \n\nThis requirement verifies that a certification path to an accepted trust anchor is used to for certificate validation and that the path includes status information. Path validation is necessary for a relying party to make an informed trust decision when presented with any certificate not already explicitly trusted. Status information for certification paths includes certificate revocation lists or online certificate status protocol responses.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-95635",
            "V-80925"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000185"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2896",
          "uuid": "faab1f16-8984-52d9-8a3e-820a6afe10b7",
          "stig_uuid": "48d52574-4cd5-5714-881e-63304d1f3ea9",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual identity provider ships an official DoD-approved externalPKI trustchain bundle built under pinnedSHA256 verification, excluding expired, email and software-signing certificates; init sync installs those certs into Keycloak truststores. This is concrete approvedCA/trustanchor source coverage, rather than an unspecified customer truststore. Operator-added UDS roots and KubernetesCA used for federatedJWT remain separate approved-boundary residuals.\n\nComponents: UDS Core 1.14 shared Keycloak realm and Horizon OIDC clients; no bundled application Keycloak\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-aaa-release] re-aaa-release\nSuccessful UDS publication artifact horizon-release-2026.45.4-uds.1 platform-contract.json selects identity.mode=uds, issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds, Core1.14.0; build-info.json enumerates12 deliveredimages and no bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-aaa-ca-build] re-aaa-ca-build\nBuild imports official unclass-dod_approved_external_pkis_trust_chains.zip only after pinned SHA256 verification; excludes EMAIL/SW authorities and expiredcertificates.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/truststore/ca-to-jks.sh\n\n[re-aaa-ca-image] re-aaa-ca-image\nIdentity config image builds official DoDCA trust bundle/cert directory and copies it into delivered image, along with realm/theme/plugins.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/Dockerfile\n\n[re-aaa-ca-sync] re-aaa-ca-sync\nConfig init sync copies certs into /opt/keycloak/conf/truststores plus realm/plugin/theme; when FIPS enabled also copies FIPS libraries. Thus approvedCA defaults have executable source, not an unspecified empty trust mechanism.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/sync.sh\n\n[re-aaa-values] re-aaa-values\nCore uses identity-config0.32, X509/OTP enabled, username/password enabled; X509_MFA false, inactivity flag unset, detailed-observability alertingdisabled. Insecure admin password generation disabled. Truststore includes UDS bundle and serviceaccount CA separately from configimage truststore.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Actual identity provider ships an official DoD-approved externalPKI trustchain bundle built under pinnedSHA256 verification, excluding expired, email and software-signing certificates; init sync installs those certs into Keycloak truststores. This is concrete approvedCA/trustanchor source coverage, rather than an unspecified customer truststore. Operator-added UDS roots and KubernetesCA used for federatedJWT remain separate approved-boundary residuals.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
