          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000185"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2896",
          "uuid": "9b65c964-7f81-54b0-9312-725aa4706728",
          "stig_uuid": "48d52574-4cd5-5714-881e-63304d1f3ea9",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual UDS dod-cac validator defaults OCSP checking=true and OCSP fail-open=false with timestamp validation. This provides revoked-certificate rejection on the actual IdP; localCRL caching is a separate rule and does not negate this online revocation mechanism.\n\nComponents: UDS Core 1.14 shared Keycloak realm and Horizon OIDC clients; no bundled application Keycloak\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-aaa-release] re-aaa-release\nSuccessful UDS publication artifact horizon-release-2026.45.4-uds.1 platform-contract.json selects identity.mode=uds, issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds, Core1.14.0; build-info.json enumerates12 deliveredimages and no bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-aaa-realm] re-aaa-realm\nRealm has failureFactor3, permanentLockout true, maxTemporaryLockouts0; password length15,upper/lower/digit,special2,history5 and expiry60days; jsonlog listener emits user/admin events. Browser has password and X509 alternative authentication; X509MFA defaultdisabled. Account-inactivity workflow optionaldisabled; no minimum-age/difference/blacklist policy selected. Recovery flow requires reset-password.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Actual UDS dod-cac validator defaults OCSP checking=true and OCSP fail-open=false with timestamp validation. This provides revoked-certificate rejection on the actual IdP; localCRL caching is a separate rule and does not negate this online revocation mechanism.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204677",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204677",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000176",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204677",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000176",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204677r961041_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204677r961041",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000176-AAA-000590",
          "rule_title": "AAA Services must be configured to enforce authorized access to the corresponding private key for PKI-based authentication.",
          "fix_text": "Configure AAA Services to enforce authorized access to the corresponding private key for PKI-based authentication.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify AAA Services are configured to enforce authorized access to the corresponding private key for PKI-based authentication.\n\nIf AAA Services are not configured to enforce authorized access to the corresponding private key, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "AAA_Services_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "If the private key is discovered, an attacker can use the key to authenticate as an authorized user and gain access to the network infrastructure.\n\nThe cornerstone of the PKI is the private key used to encrypt or digitally sign information. \n\nIf the private key is stolen, this will lead to the compromise of the authentication and non-repudiation gained through PKI because the attacker can use the private key to digitally sign documents and pretend to be the authorized user. \n\nBoth the holders of a digital certificate and the issuing authority must protect the computers, storage devices, or whatever they use to keep the private keys.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-95639",
            "V-80929"
          ],
          "ccis": [
