          ],
          "group_id": "V-204693",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000394",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204693r961503_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204693r961503",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000394-AAA-000430",
          "rule_title": "AAA Services used for 802.1x must be configured to authenticate network endpoint devices (supplicants) before the authenticator establishes any connection.",
          "fix_text": "Configure AAA Services to authenticate supplicants before the authenticator establishes any connection.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If AAA Services are not used for 802.1x endpoint identification and authentication, this is not applicable.\n\nVerify AAA Services are configured to authenticate supplicants before the authenticator establishes any connection.\n\nIf AAA Services are not configured to authenticate supplicants before the authenticator establishes any connection, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "AAA_Services_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Without authenticating devices, unidentified or unknown devices may be introduced, thereby facilitating malicious activity.\n\nFor distributed architectures (e.g., service-oriented architectures), the decisions regarding the validation of authentication claims may be made by services separate from the services acting on those decisions. In such situations, it is necessary to provide authentication decisions (as opposed to the actual authenticators) to the services that need to act on those decisions.\n\nThis requirement applies to applications that connect either locally, remotely, or through a network to an endpoint device (including but not limited to workstations, printers, servers [outside a datacenter], VoIP phones, VTC CODECs). Gateways and SOA applications are examples of where this requirement would apply. \n\nDevice authentication is a solution enabling an organization to manage devices. It is an additional layer of authentication ensuring only specific pre-authorized devices can access the system.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-95609",
            "V-80899"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001958"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2896",
          "uuid": "7a98e384-73a5-5049-95d9-cdaf8ca4b5f1",
          "stig_uuid": "48d52574-4cd5-5714-881e-63304d1f3ea9",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs N/A justification\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The assessed delivered AAA component is shared Keycloak OIDC web/CLI identity, not an 802.1x authenticator, RADIUS/TACACS+ device-login service or VLAN assignment service. These exact checks condition applicability on those network-access roles. Record that provider/component scope and assess actual network authenticators separately; do not build EAP or guest VLAN behavior into Keycloak.\n\nComponents: UDS Core 1.14 shared Keycloak realm and Horizon OIDC clients; no bundled application Keycloak\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Record the network-AAA role exclusion in component applicability; reassess any separate 802.1x/device-management service.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-aaa-release] re-aaa-release\nSuccessful UDS publication artifact horizon-release-2026.45.4-uds.1 platform-contract.json selects identity.mode=uds, issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds, Core1.14.0; build-info.json enumerates12 deliveredimages and no bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-aaa-realm] re-aaa-realm\nRealm has failureFactor3, permanentLockout true, maxTemporaryLockouts0; password length15,upper/lower/digit,special2,history5 and expiry60days; jsonlog listener emits user/admin events. Browser has password and X509 alternative authentication; X509MFA defaultdisabled. Account-inactivity workflow optionaldisabled; no minimum-age/difference/blacklist policy selected. Recovery flow requires reset-password.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-aaa-sso-boundary] re-aaa-sso-boundary\nPlatformKeycloak admin/public ingress is separate, sanitized clientcertificate header forwarded from gateway; certificate access available but optional at default browser boundary.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Needs N/A justification. The assessed delivered AAA component is shared Keycloak OIDC web/CLI identity, not an 802.1x authenticator, RADIUS/TACACS+ device-login service or VLAN assignment service. These exact checks condition applicability on those network-access roles. Record that provider/component scope and assess actual network authenticators separately; do not build EAP or guest VLAN behavior into Keycloak.\n\nNext action: Record the network-AAA role exclusion in component applicability; reassess any separate 802.1x/device-management service."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204695",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204695",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000516",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204695",
          "severity": "low",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000516",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204695r981588_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204695r981588",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000516-AAA-000350",
          "rule_title": "AAA Services must be configured to use at least two NTP servers to synchronize time.",
          "fix_text": "Configure AAA Services to use two separate NTP servers. Both a primary and backup NTP server must be identified in the configuration.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify AAA Services are configured to use at least two NTP servers to synchronize time. Both a primary and backup NTP server must be identified in the configuration. AAA Services may leverage the capability of an operating system.\n\nIf AAA Services are not configured to use at least two separate NTP servers, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "AAA_Services_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Inaccurate time stamps make it more difficult to correlate events and can lead to an inaccurate analysis. Determining the correct time a particular event occurred on a system is critical when conducting forensic analysis and investigating system events. Sources outside of the configured acceptable allowance (drift) may be inaccurate. Additionally, unnecessary synchronization may have an adverse impact on system performance and may indicate malicious activity. \n\nIf the internal clock is not used, the system may not be able to provide time stamps for log messages. Additionally, externally generated time stamps may not be accurate. Applications can use the capability of an operating system or purpose-built module for this purpose. Synchronizing the internal clock using NTP provides uniformity for all system clocks over a network. NTP provides an efficient and scalable method for network devices to synchronize to an accurate time source.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-95593",
            "V-80883"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004923"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2896",
          "uuid": "ce9e3a1b-3663-5014-9f51-8db2588c0e25",
          "stig_uuid": "48d52574-4cd5-5714-881e-63304d1f3ea9",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The exact check permits leveraging the operating system. Keycloak containers use the node clock and do not originate an independent NTP client configuration; MPN owns node OS/time service under the scope. Recommend likely inherited coverage, conditional on the MPN host checklist establishing at least two configured primary/backup time sources. Source does not assert that unseen server count was observed.\n\nComponents: UDS Core 1.14 shared Keycloak realm and Horizon OIDC clients; no bundled application Keycloak\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Attach MPN node-OS inheritance/checklist for two distinct NTP sources; no Keycloak-local NTP daemon is needed.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-aaa-release] re-aaa-release\nSuccessful UDS publication artifact horizon-release-2026.45.4-uds.1 platform-contract.json selects identity.mode=uds, issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds, Core1.14.0; build-info.json enumerates12 deliveredimages and no bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN\n\n[re-aaa-json-events] re-aaa-json-events\nListener serializes user/admin identity events to stdout with actor/source/time/detail, suitable for UDS Vector collection; does not itself pushSA/ISSO notification messages.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The exact check permits leveraging the operating system. Keycloak containers use the node clock and do not originate an independent NTP client configuration; MPN owns node OS/time service under the scope. Recommend likely inherited coverage, conditional on the MPN host checklist establishing at least two configured primary/backup time sources. Source does not assert that unseen server count was observed.\n\nNext action: Attach MPN node-OS inheritance/checklist for two distinct NTP sources; no Keycloak-local NTP daemon is needed."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204696",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204696",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000516",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204696",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000516",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204696r981589_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204696r981589",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000516-AAA-000360",
          "rule_title": "AAA Services must be configured to authenticate all NTP messages received from NTP servers and peers.",
          "fix_text": "Configure AAA Services to authenticate all received NTP messages using a FIPS-approved message authentication code algorithm. When AAA Services are not capable of using FIPS-approved message authentication code algorithms, configure AAA Services to use MD5 message authentication code algorithms.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify AAA Services are configured to authenticate all NTP messages received from NTP servers and peers. \n\nThe NTP server or peer authentication must use a FIPS-approved message authentication code algorithm. FIPS-approved algorithms for authentication are the cipher-based message authentication code (CMAC) and the keyed-hash message authentication code (HMAC). AES and 3DES are NIST-approved CMAC algorithms. The following are NIST-approved HMAC algorithms: SHA-1, SHA-224, SHA-256, SHA-384, SHA-512, SHA-512/224, and SHA-512/256. AAA Services may leverage the capability of an operating system.\n\nIf AAA Services are not configured to authenticate all NTP messages using a FIPS-approved message authentication code algorithm, this is a finding.\n\nIf AAA Services are not capable of authenticating the NTP server or peer using a FIPS-approved message authentication code algorithm, but are configured to use an MD5 for NTP message authentication, this is downgraded to a CAT III.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "AAA_Services_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Inaccurate time stamps make it more difficult to correlate events and can lead to an inaccurate analysis. Determining the correct time a particular event occurred on a system is critical when conducting forensic analysis and investigating system events. Sources outside of the configured acceptable allowance (drift) may be inaccurate. Additionally, unnecessary synchronization may have an adverse impact on system performance and may indicate malicious activity. \n\nSynchronizing internal information system clocks provides uniformity of time stamps for information systems with multiple system clocks and systems connected over a network. NTP provides an efficient and scalable method for network devices to synchronize to an accurate time source. NTP may pose a security risk if a malicious user were able to falsify NTP information. To launch an attack on the NTP infrastructure, a hacker could inject time that would be accepted by NTP clients by spoofing the IP address of a valid NTP server. To mitigate this risk, the time messages must be authenticated by the client before accepting them as a time source. \n\nTwo NTP-enabled devices can communicate in either client-server mode or peer-to-peer mode (aka \"symmetric mode\"). The peering mode is configured manually on the device and indicated in the outgoing NTP packets. The fundamental difference is the synchronization behavior: an NTP server can synchronize to a peer with better stratum, whereas it will never synchronize to its client regardless of the client's stratum. From a protocol perspective, NTP clients are no different from the NTP servers. The NTP client can synchronize to multiple NTP servers, select the best server and synchronize with it, or synchronize to the averaged value returned by the servers.\n\nA hierarchical model can be used to improve scalability. With this implementation, an NTP client can also become an NTP server providing time to downstream clients at a higher stratum level and of decreasing accuracy than that of its upstream server. To increase availability, NTP peering can be used between NTP servers. In the event the device loses connectivity to its upstream NTP server, it will be able to choose time from one of its peers. \n\nThe NTP authentication model is opposite of the typical client-server authentication model. NTP authentication enables an NTP client or peer to authenticate time received from their servers and peers. It is not used to authenticate NTP clients because NTP servers do not care about the authenticity of their clients, as they never accept any time from them.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-95595",
            "V-80885"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004923"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2896",
          "uuid": "c4c9143c-9a5c-54ba-946c-2dfd6b283156",
          "stig_uuid": "48d52574-4cd5-5714-881e-63304d1f3ea9",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: NTP message authentication belongs to the MPN node operating system/time service used by Keycloak containers, as expressly permitted by this check. Recommend likely inherited coverage with the FIPS-approved MAC requirement retained for the host boundary; UTC event formatting alone does not prove authenticated NTP.\n\nComponents: UDS Core 1.14 shared Keycloak realm and Horizon OIDC clients; no bundled application Keycloak\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Attach MPN host-time authentication inheritance evidence specifying the permitted MAC algorithm and peers.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-aaa-release] re-aaa-release\nSuccessful UDS publication artifact horizon-release-2026.45.4-uds.1 platform-contract.json selects identity.mode=uds, issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds, Core1.14.0; build-info.json enumerates12 deliveredimages and no bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN\n\n[re-aaa-json-events] re-aaa-json-events\nListener serializes user/admin identity events to stdout with actor/source/time/detail, suitable for UDS Vector collection; does not itself pushSA/ISSO notification messages.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. NTP message authentication belongs to the MPN node operating system/time service used by Keycloak containers, as expressly permitted by this check. Recommend likely inherited coverage with the FIPS-approved MAC requirement retained for the host boundary; UTC event formatting alone does not prove authenticated NTP.\n\nNext action: Attach MPN host-time authentication inheritance evidence specifying the permitted MAC algorithm and peers."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204697",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204697",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000516",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204697",
          "severity": "low",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000516",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204697r961863_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204697r961863",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000516-AAA-000370",
          "rule_title": "AAA Services must be configured to use their loopback or OOB management interface address as the source address when originating NTP traffic.",
          "fix_text": "Configure AAA Services to use their loopback or OOB management interface address as the source address when originating NTP traffic.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify AAA Services are configured to use their loopback interface address as the source address when originating NTP traffic. When AAA Services are managed from an OOB management network, the OOB interface must be used instead of the loopback address for originating NTP traffic.\n\nIf AAA Services are not configured to use the OOB interface when managed from an OOB management network, this is a finding.\n\nIf AAA Services are not configured to use the loopback or OOB management interface as the source address when originating NTP traffic, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "AAA_Services_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Inaccurate time stamps make it more difficult to correlate events and can lead to an inaccurate analysis. Determining the correct time a particular event occurred on a system is critical when conducting forensic analysis and investigating system events. Sources outside of the configured acceptable allowance (drift) may be inaccurate. Additionally, unnecessary synchronization may have an adverse impact on system performance and may indicate malicious activity. \n\nSynchronizing internal information system clocks provides uniformity of time stamps for information systems with multiple system clocks and systems connected over a network. NTP provides an efficient and scalable method for network devices to synchronize to an accurate time source.\n\nUsing a loopback address as the source address offers a multitude of uses for security, access, management, and scalability of routers. It is easier to construct appropriate ingress filters for router management plane traffic destined to the network management subnet since the source addresses will be from the range used for loopback interfaces instead of a larger range of addresses used for physical interfaces. Log information recorded by authentication and syslog servers will record the router's loopback address instead of the numerous physical interface addresses. NTP messages sent to management servers should use the loopback address as the source address.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-95597",
            "V-80887"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000366"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2896",
          "uuid": "6cdf07de-ab73-517d-8b55-fe201127e97b",
          "stig_uuid": "48d52574-4cd5-5714-881e-63304d1f3ea9",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The identity container inherits host time and does not originate separate NTP traffic. Any loopback/OOB source-interface requirement therefore applies to the MPN host time client/management topology. Recommend likely inherited coverage and retain that specific host configuration item instead of adding network-device settings to Keycloak.\n\nComponents: UDS Core 1.14 shared Keycloak realm and Horizon OIDC clients; no bundled application Keycloak\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Attach the MPN host-time source-interface/OOB management inheritance mapping.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-aaa-release] re-aaa-release\nSuccessful UDS publication artifact horizon-release-2026.45.4-uds.1 platform-contract.json selects identity.mode=uds, issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds, Core1.14.0; build-info.json enumerates12 deliveredimages and no bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN\n\n[re-aaa-json-events] re-aaa-json-events\nListener serializes user/admin identity events to stdout with actor/source/time/detail, suitable for UDS Vector collection; does not itself pushSA/ISSO notification messages.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The identity container inherits host time and does not originate separate NTP traffic. Any loopback/OOB source-interface requirement therefore applies to the MPN host time client/management topology. Recommend likely inherited coverage and retain that specific host configuration item instead of adding network-device settings to Keycloak.\n\nNext action: Attach the MPN host-time source-interface/OOB management inheritance mapping."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204698",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204698",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000516",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204698",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000516",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204698r1267977_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204698r1267977",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000516-AAA-000440",
          "rule_title": "AAA Services used for 802.1x must be configured to use secure Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP), such as EAP-TLS, EAP-TTLS, and PEAP.",
          "fix_text": "Configure AAA Services used for 802.1x to use secure EAP, such as EAP-TLS, EAP-TTLS, and PEAP.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify AAA Services used for 802.1x are configured to use secure EAP. Currently acceptable secure protocols are EAP-TLS, EAP-TTLS, and PEAP.\n\nIf AAA Services used for 802.1x are not configured to use secure EAP, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "AAA_Services_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Additional new EAP methods/types are still being proposed. However, the three being considered secure are EAP-TLS, EAP-TTLS, and PEAP. PEAP is the preferred EAP type to be used in DoW for its ability to support a greater number of operating systems and its capability to transmit statement of health information, per NSA NAC study.\n\nLightweight EAP (LEAP) is a CISCO proprietary protocol providing an easy-to-deploy one-password authentication. LEAP is vulnerable to dictionary attacks. A \"man in the middle\" can capture traffic, identify a password, and then use it to access a WLAN. LEAP is inappropriate and does not provide sufficient security for use on DoW networks.\n\nEAP-MD5 is functionally similar to CHAP and is susceptible to eavesdropping because the password credentials are sent as a hash (not encrypted). In addition, server administrators would be required to store unencrypted passwords on their servers violating other security policies. EAP-MD5 is inappropriate and does not provide sufficient security for use on DoW networks.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-95611",
            "V-80901"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000366"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2896",
          "uuid": "d86e4519-ddc5-57da-b043-84f068c6ffc0",
          "stig_uuid": "48d52574-4cd5-5714-881e-63304d1f3ea9",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs N/A justification\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The assessed delivered AAA component is shared Keycloak OIDC web/CLI identity, not an 802.1x authenticator, RADIUS/TACACS+ device-login service or VLAN assignment service. These exact checks condition applicability on those network-access roles. Record that provider/component scope and assess actual network authenticators separately; do not build EAP or guest VLAN behavior into Keycloak.\n\nComponents: UDS Core 1.14 shared Keycloak realm and Horizon OIDC clients; no bundled application Keycloak\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Record the network-AAA role exclusion in component applicability; reassess any separate 802.1x/device-management service.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-aaa-release] re-aaa-release\nSuccessful UDS publication artifact horizon-release-2026.45.4-uds.1 platform-contract.json selects identity.mode=uds, issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds, Core1.14.0; build-info.json enumerates12 deliveredimages and no bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-aaa-realm] re-aaa-realm\nRealm has failureFactor3, permanentLockout true, maxTemporaryLockouts0; password length15,upper/lower/digit,special2,history5 and expiry60days; jsonlog listener emits user/admin events. Browser has password and X509 alternative authentication; X509MFA defaultdisabled. Account-inactivity workflow optionaldisabled; no minimum-age/difference/blacklist policy selected. Recovery flow requires reset-password.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-aaa-sso-boundary] re-aaa-sso-boundary\nPlatformKeycloak admin/public ingress is separate, sanitized clientcertificate header forwarded from gateway; certificate access available but optional at default browser boundary.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Needs N/A justification. The assessed delivered AAA component is shared Keycloak OIDC web/CLI identity, not an 802.1x authenticator, RADIUS/TACACS+ device-login service or VLAN assignment service. These exact checks condition applicability on those network-access roles. Record that provider/component scope and assess actual network authenticators separately; do not build EAP or guest VLAN behavior into Keycloak.\n\nNext action: Record the network-AAA role exclusion in component applicability; reassess any separate 802.1x/device-management service."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204699",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204699",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000516",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204699",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000516",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204699r961863_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204699r961863",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000516-AAA-000620",
          "rule_title": "AAA Services must not be configured with shared accounts.",
          "fix_text": "Configure AAA Services with no shared accounts. Remove all group profiles.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If AAA Services rely on directory services for user account management, this is not applicable and the connected directory services must perform this function.\n\nVerify AAA Services are not configured with shared accounts. Identify group profile definitions that do not meet the accounts user-id naming convention. \n\nBelow is a super-user example of how an SA profile may be associated.\n\nGroup Profile Information\ngroup = super-user{\nprofile_id = 40\nprofile_cycle = 1\nservice=shell {\ndefault cmd=permit\ncmd=debug {\ndeny all\npermit .*\n}\n}\n}\n\nBelow is an example of the user definition that should be assigned with a valid ID (not rtr-geek). Look for group accounts here:\n\nuser = rtr-geek{\nprofile_id = 45\nprofile_cycle = 1\nmember = rtr_super\npassword = des \"********\"\n}\n\nIf AAA Services are configured with shared accounts (group profiles), this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "AAA_Services_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Shared accounts configured for use on a network device do not allow for accountability or repudiation of individuals using them. If shared accounts are not changed when someone leaves the group, that person could possibly gain control of the network device. Having shared accounts does not allow for proper auditing of who is accessing or changing the network. For this reason, shared accounts are not permitted.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-95645",
            "V-80935"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000366"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2896",
          "uuid": "8cf2aeea-ddfd-5dbd-a005-4ce8dc9d3cc0",
          "stig_uuid": "48d52574-4cd5-5714-881e-63304d1f3ea9",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Delivered Horizon uses sharedUDS individual identities and tenantgroup membership; no bundled bootstrapadmin/user database is delivered. Core insecureAdminPasswordGeneration is disabled by default. Groups implement authorization and are not shared authenticators. Actual named admin assignment remains an operator access-review record, not evidence that source configures a shared account.\n\nComponents: UDS Core 1.14 shared Keycloak realm and Horizon OIDC clients; no bundled application Keycloak\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-aaa-release] re-aaa-release\nSuccessful UDS publication artifact horizon-release-2026.45.4-uds.1 platform-contract.json selects identity.mode=uds, issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds, Core1.14.0; build-info.json enumerates12 deliveredimages and no bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-aaa-values] re-aaa-values\nCore uses identity-config0.32, X509/OTP enabled, username/password enabled; X509_MFA false, inactivity flag unset, detailed-observability alertingdisabled. Insecure admin password generation disabled. Truststore includes UDS bundle and serviceaccount CA separately from configimage truststore.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[re-aaa-realm] re-aaa-realm\nRealm has failureFactor3, permanentLockout true, maxTemporaryLockouts0; password length15,upper/lower/digit,special2,history5 and expiry60days; jsonlog listener emits user/admin events. Browser has password and X509 alternative authentication; X509MFA defaultdisabled. Account-inactivity workflow optionaldisabled; no minimum-age/difference/blacklist policy selected. Recovery flow requires reset-password.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Delivered Horizon uses sharedUDS individual identities and tenantgroup membership; no bundled bootstrapadmin/user database is delivered. Core insecureAdminPasswordGeneration is disabled by default. Groups implement authorization and are not shared authenticators. Actual named admin assignment remains an operator access-review record, not evidence that source configures a shared account.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204700",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204700",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000516",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-204700",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000516",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-204700r961863_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-204700r961863",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000516-AAA-000630",
          "rule_title": "AAA Services used to authenticate privileged users for device management must be configured to connect to the management network.",
          "fix_text": "Configure AAA Services used to authenticate privileged users for device management to connect to the management network.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If AAA Services are not used for authentication of privileged users to AAA Services, this is not applicable.\n\nVerify AAA Services are configured to connect to the management network. Confirm AAA Services are not dual-homed by physically inspecting the physical LAN connection.\n\nIf AAA Services are configured to connect to a non-management network, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "AAA_Services_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Using standardized authentication protocols such as RADIUS, TACACS+, and Kerberos, an authentication server provides centralized and robust authentication services for the management of network components. In order to control access to the servers as well as monitor traffic to them, the authentication servers should only be connected to the management network.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-80937",
            "SV-95647"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000366"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2896",
          "uuid": "3069cd9d-e0cf-5e8b-acb2-da284f30e23b",
          "stig_uuid": "48d52574-4cd5-5714-881e-63304d1f3ea9",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs N/A justification\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: These checks concern AAA used for 802.1x or privileged network-device management, with physical/VLAN management-segment assumptions. Delivered shared Keycloak serves OIDC application identity and supplies a separate admin gateway/logical namespace boundary. Record the network-device AAA role exclusion; if an assessor applies the requirement to IdP administration, use that actual logical separation and inherited host network topology as tailoring evidence.\n\nComponents: UDS Core 1.14 shared Keycloak realm and Horizon OIDC clients; no bundled application Keycloak\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Record assessed AAA role and management-boundary tailoring; assess any separate network-device AAA service.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-aaa-release] re-aaa-release\nSuccessful UDS publication artifact horizon-release-2026.45.4-uds.1 platform-contract.json selects identity.mode=uds, issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds, Core1.14.0; build-info.json enumerates12 deliveredimages and no bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-aaa-sso-boundary] re-aaa-sso-boundary\nPlatformKeycloak admin/public ingress is separate, sanitized clientcertificate header forwarded from gateway; certificate access available but optional at default browser boundary.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Needs N/A justification. These checks concern AAA used for 802.1x or privileged network-device management, with physical/VLAN management-segment assumptions. Delivered shared Keycloak serves OIDC application identity and supplies a separate admin gateway/logical namespace boundary. Record the network-device AAA role exclusion; if an assessor applies the requirement to IdP administration, use that actual logical separation and inherited host network topology as tailoring evidence.\n\nNext action: Record assessed AAA role and management-boundary tailoring; assess any separate network-device AAA service."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-204701",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-204701",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000516",
